INFINITE-TOURNAMENT HYPOTHESIS
==============================
One useful infinite tournament is not on integers themselves but on certificate states.
A vertex can be (f, local data retained): fixed divisor, residue obstructions,
least Singh/Cohn certificate depth, trace-subset survivor set, Newton polygon edge data.
Orient A -> B when A has the smaller retained certificate depth after normalizing degree
and fixed divisor; orient ties by the richer side channel.  Finite truncations then
record edge flips as the prime window grows.  This makes Bunyakovsky a statement
about an infinite supply of forward prime-hit witnesses, while Singh/Cohn/Iravanian
supply reverse and algorithmic edges in the same tournament.

ASSUMPTION CHALLENGE
====================
Alternate vertex sets considered:
  polynomials; integer values; primes; residues modulo p; fixed-divisor obstructions;
  roots; real/complex conjugate blocks; recombination subsets; digit positions;
  Newton polygon edges; bivariate specialization channels; LRC runners/gaps;
  codewords, matroid circuits, minimum-design blocks, and proof obligations.
Chosen vertices: proof carriers.
Preserved: the two-way prime/irreducible direction, support retention,
computability, and transfer potential to LRC14 and [72,36,16].
Destroyed: individual polynomial arithmetic, actual asymptotic prime production,
root geometry beyond first trace, and code-support realizability.
Challenged assumption: the tournament need not live on runners, arcs, or primes;
it can live on certificate channels and still preserve the obstruction predicate.
